Yes. I think Local Prime is exactly the right family of idea.

Some “whys” are useful because they are not globally exposed. Their function can depend on remaining tacit, contextual, or selectively legible. Naming them can change the system around them: people start gaming them, overfitting to them, anchoring on them, or treating the label as the thing itself.

So the right architecture is not “hide everything.” It is:

understood cause → sealed provenance → bounded interface → selective disclosure

That preserves the reason without forcing it into every layer.

The analogy to a prime works because the unit is irreducible relative to that local context. You can wield it without decomposing it every time. But because it has potential, it also has dual-use properties: the same pattern can stabilize or destabilize, protect or manipulate, depending on who invokes it and for what purpose.

The safeguard is that tacit does not become unauditable. There should still be a recoverable path back to the underlying why when stakes require it. Otherwise “protected wisdom” can quietly become “unexamined assumption.”

So I’d distinguish:

Local Prime — high-leverage, context-bound unit.
Protected Why — explanation deliberately not surfaced by default.
Sealed Provenance — the underlying derivation remains recoverable.
Selective Invocation — use only where the local conditions justify it.

That also fits your point about some things being damaged by observation or titling. Sometimes the best interface is to preserve the function and leave the symbol asleep until it is needed.